            if (New_Ret)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The test method starts by assuming a precondition for the unit under test (`arrList`), that is guaranteed by the Pex engine, to be true. After that, it creates an instance of an ArrayList object, stores its old size, remembers the index of x in the old list. It uses unit under test to add x to ArrayList and then checks whether the updated list maintains the correctness of the index of x.

The important part of the test is the assertion at the end, which is made based on the assumption that if the return value of the container's Contains method is true, then the index of x in the updated list or array is bigger or equal to zero.

In the provided test, Pex engine automatically generated different values for `arrList` and `x`. It also automatically reused values if possible, which leads to a smaller number of generated test cases. This is the key to achieve a high code coverage rate.

For more advanced usage, like testing the thread safety of the ArrayList, advanced fuzzing and mutation testing could be performed. This provides a deeper understanding of the behavior of the ArrayList under different scenarios. However, the testing of specific functionalities like sorting, binary search etc. goes beyond the scope of this introduction and would require additional code examples.

Please note that if the 'arrList' object is not actually under test, the unit under test here would be the ArrayList class itself (in which the Contains method is defined), and not the unit under test specified in the method name. The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute [PexAssumeUnderTest] means that we are assuming that the ArrayList in the test is the actual target unit under test. You can replace this by the real unit under test if you wish. 

To use Pex with a more complex unit under test (e.g. a class with multiple methods), you would also need to use a more complex test method (which might create additional inputs, outputs, and side effects of the unit under test, as well as assertions). Pex's coverage goals would need to be carefully designed to ensure a meaningful test is being generated.

To use Pex with a console application instead of a test method, you can do so by right-clicking the method or the class and then selecting 'Test with Managed Code Coverage' or 'Test with Native Code Coverage'. 

As with all automated tests, manual review and debugging may be necessary to validate correctness and ensure that the test correctly replicates and verifies the intended behavior.

This is a basic introduction to how Pex can be used to perform automated unit testing. More detailed examples, in-depth testing strategies and best practices would be beyond the scope of this introduction. Yet, the examples and documentation provided should assist you in implementing effective automated testing strategies using Pex.
