```

The property `[PexAssumeUnderTest]` makes this parameter a target for an analysis performed by the test engine. The test engine uses this knowledge to design new test cases and to verify the correctness of the unit under test.

The method `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true)` makes the assumption that the previous call to the `Contains` method produced `true`.

The `PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);` is used to display the property values which can be used by testing tools to generate more tests or report the progress of testing.

The `Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0, New_Ret);` is used for verification step, this test checks that the outcome depends on whether x could be found in the array list or not at the step of invoking `Contains(x)`.

Please note, running such tests may significantly increase the run-time of your test cases if the test data size is large. To control the size of the test data used in Pex-generated tests, you can use [PexMethod(MaxConstraints = new int[] { ... })] to specify your constraints.

Also, to enable Pex-generated code cover measurement, remember to link to Microsoft.Pex.Framework. This is done usually in the "Additional Dependencies" of your project's properties. 

Please be aware that Pex (Microsoft Path Testing) requires a license to be purchased and installed. It is a commercial tool, and the community version is limited for non-commercial use. For an open-source project or project of small size, you can consider to evaluate it first.

More details could be found from the official Microsoft Pex documents. If any issues occur during using Pex, you may want to refer to the detailed information or discuss in the community forums.

This approach is a kind of “Pex-first” approach, i.e., you start writing tests with Pex and then refactor the code for its readability and maintainability as well for manually written tests. Pex is a fantastic aid to make tests more reliable and more robust by exploring potential inputs intelligently and can be also integrated into a larger test suite as a part of continuous integration. For a .NET open-source project, the benefit can be significantly enhanced that your tests have already helped other developers test your code. 

